```
SYSTEM OVERVIEW
===============
The system is a Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) system supporting multiple users, role hierarchy, temporary role delegation, revocation of delegated roles, and concurrent request processing. Users have security clearances (High, Medium, Low) and interact with system objects, each with a classification level. Actions are permitted based on clearances, roles, and object classifications. Administrators have High clearance, but not all High-clearance users are administrators. The system enforces authorization correctness, permission consistency, request liveness, and concurrency safety. The new Emergency Module adds special system states and behaviors during emergencies, altering request processing, role exclusivity, and administrator privileges. Emergency mode can be triggered only once per session and requires exactly two administrators to simultaneously initiate the transition.

PROSPECTIVE FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
=======================
R1: Emergency Mode Triggering
While in Normal mode, exactly two administrators are required to simultaneously trigger the transition to Emergency mode. Emergency mode can only be triggered once per session.

R2: Emergency Mode Duration and Exit
Emergency mode is active only for a limited (fixed) period; the system must eventually return to Normal mode.

R3: Emergency Mode Behavior
3.1: During Emergency mode, request processing may bypass FIFO ordering to prioritize and process multiple high-classification requests concurrently.
3.2: During Emergency mode, users may temporarily hold roles that are otherwise mutually exclusive. Mutual exclusivity is determined by a static, predetermined set of mutually exclusive role pairs.
3.3: During Emergency mode, administrators temporarily hold emergency privileges.

R4: Emergency Mode Exit Actions
4.1: Upon exit from Emergency mode, all emergency privileges granted to administrators must be revoked.
4.2: All roles delegated specifically during Emergency mode must be revoked immediately upon exit to Normal mode. Delegated roles existing from before Emergency mode are managed according to their standard expiry or may be revoked early by an administrator.
4.3: Administrators must update their credentials after Emergency mode ends.

KEY CONCEPTS AND ENTITIES
==========================
- User: Entity with a clearance level and roles (assigned, delegated).
- Administrator: User with High clearance and special privileges.
- Role: Assigned to users; may have mutual exclusivity and minimum clearance requirements. Mutually exclusive roles are defined by a static set of role pairs.
- Delegated Role: Temporary assignment of a role from one user to another.
- Security Clearance: High, Medium, or Low level assigned to users.
- System Object: Entity affected by requests; each has a classification level (High, Medium, Low).
- Request: Action submitted by a user, affecting system objects.
- Request Classification: Highest classification level among affected objects.
- Emergency Mode: Special system state entered by a simultaneous trigger action by exactly two administrators, with altered behaviors. Can only be triggered once per session.
- Normal Mode: Default system state.
- Emergency Privileges: Special privileges granted to administrators during Emergency mode.
- Mutual Exclusive Roles: Roles that cannot be held simultaneously in Normal mode. These are static, predetermined pairs.
- Emergency Trigger: Joint simultaneous action by two administrators to enter Emergency mode.
- Emergency Duration: The fixed period during which Emergency mode remains active.

ASSUMPTIONS REQUIRING CLARIFICATION
====================================
[All previous points have been clarified by the user.]
(No outstanding assumptions requiring clarification.)

CONSTRAINTS
===========
- Users must not submit requests classified above their clearance level.
- Delegated roles are limited by clearance and must expire or be revocable.
- Request processing is FIFO in Normal mode; may be prioritized for high-classification requests in Emergency mode.
- Mutual exclusivity of roles is enforced in Normal mode and temporarily relaxed in Emergency mode, based on a static, predetermined set of mutually exclusive role pairs.
- Emergency mode must be triggered by exactly two administrators acting simultaneously.
- Emergency mode is time-limited and must eventually terminate.
- Emergency mode can only be triggered once per session.
- All emergency privileges and roles delegated specifically during Emergency mode must be revoked upon exit.
- Delegated roles existing from before Emergency mode are managed as per standard expiry or revocation rules.
- Administrators must update credentials immediately after Emergency mode ends.
